### 🚨 CRASH DEBUGGING

#### Crash Log Analysis
```swift
// Reading crash logs:
// Xcode → Window → Devices and Simulators → View Device Logs

// Crash log sections:
// 1. Exception Type (what crashed)
// 2. Exception Codes (memory address, reason)
// 3. Thread State (registers at crash)
// 4. Thread Backtrace (call stack)
// 5. Binary Images (loaded frameworks)

// Common crash types:

// ❌ EXC_BAD_ACCESS (SIGSEGV)
// Cause: Accessing deallocated memory
var user: User? = User()
user = nil
// print(user!.name)  // Crash! Accessing nil

// Fix: Optional handling
if let user = user {
    print(user.name)
}

// ❌ EXC_CRASH (SIGABRT)
// Cause: Force unwrap nil, assert, precondition
// let name = user!.name  // Crash if user is nil

// Fix: Safe unwrapping
guard let user = user else { return }
let name = user.name

// ❌ EXC_BAD_INSTRUCTION (SIGILL)
// Cause: Swift runtime error (enum, bounds)
enum State {
    case loading
    case loaded
}
let rawValue = 999
// let state = State(rawValue: rawValue)!  // Crash!

// Fix: Handle invalid cases
guard let state = State(rawValue: rawValue) else {
    print("Invalid state")
    return
}

// ❌ Watchdog Timeout (0x8badf00d)
// Cause: App took too long to launch/suspend
// Fix: Move heavy work off main thread
```

#### Symbolication
```bash
# Symbolicating crash logs manually:

# 1. Get dSYM file
# Xcode → Window → Organizer → Select Archive → Show in Finder
# Right-click .xcarchive → Show Package Contents → dSYMs

# 2. Symbolicate
xcrun atos -arch arm64 -o MyChannel.app.dSYM/Contents/Resources/DWARF/MyChannel -l 0x100000000 0x123456789

# 3. Or use Xcode:
# Drag crash log to Devices window
# Xcode auto-symbolicates if dSYM available
```

#### Exception Breakpoints
```
Xcode → Breakpoint Navigator → + → Exception Breakpoint

Types:
1. All Exceptions (Objective-C)
2. Swift Error Breakpoint
3. Runtime Issues (SwiftUI)

Options:
- Break on throw (before catch)
- Break on unhandled
- Add conditions
```

