### 🧠 MEMORY DEBUGGING

#### Zombies (Detect Over-Released Objects)
```
Product → Scheme → Edit Scheme → Diagnostics

Enable:
✅ Zombie Objects
✅ Malloc Stack (Live Allocations Only)

What it does:
- Keeps deallocated objects as "zombies"
- Crash → tells you which zombie was accessed
- Shows allocation stack trace

// Example zombie crash:
// [MyClass retain]: message sent to deallocated instance
```

#### Address Sanitizer (Detect Memory Corruption)
```
Product → Scheme → Edit Scheme → Diagnostics

Enable:
✅ Address Sanitizer

Detects:
- Use after free
- Heap buffer overflow
- Stack buffer overflow
- Memory leaks
- Use after return

Trade-off:
- App runs 2-5x slower
- Uses 3x memory
- Only use in debugging
```

#### Malloc Stack Logging
```
Product → Scheme → Edit Scheme → Diagnostics

Enable:
✅ Malloc Stack
   - Live Allocations Only (normal)
   - All Allocations (for leaks)

View in Debug Navigator:
Xcode → Debug → Memory → Memory Graph

Features:
- See all allocated objects
- View allocation stack trace
- Find retain cycles
- Filter by class
```

#### Memory Graph Debugger
```
Debug → Debug Memory Graph
or: Click memory graph icon in debug bar

Features:
- See all objects in memory
- Show reference graph
- Detect cycles
- Filter by type
- Export graph

Common patterns:
1. Strong reference cycle:
   ViewModel → Closure → ViewModel

2. Delegate cycle:
   Parent → Child (strong) → Parent (delegate not weak)

3. Timer cycle:
   Object → Timer (strong target) → Object

4. NotificationCenter:
   Object → NotificationCenter → Object (observer not removed)
```

